WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.775 --> 00:00:02.400
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
Instead of trying

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.090
to convince the other person
or persuade the other person,

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.050
listen.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.230
Help them empty their heart
and express why they're

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.760
frustrated with the situation.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.500
And then they'll be
more likely to listen

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.310
to your own experiences
and more receptive to why

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.420
you think climate change
matters and is relevant.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:22.980
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.670
we're exploring climate action
and how one nonprofit is

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:30.860
providing a framework for young
people to help effect change.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.360
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: The thing
about teaching young people,

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.340
in particular, is
that young people

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.010
are open-minded to new
material, but they're

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.650
mature enough to understand
fairly complex concepts.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.020
Ultimately, we hope
that Ambassadors

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.780
will teach students,
students will teach families,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.500
and families will demand action.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.630
SARAH HANSEN: I'm your
host, Sarah Hansen.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.150
With us today is
Brandon Leshchinskiy,

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:57.090
a graduate student in a
technology and policy master's

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.030
program and the aeronautics
and astronautics master's

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.770
program at MIT.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:06.330
Brandon's also working with
a nonprofit called EarthDNA.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.130
If this organization
sounds familiar to you,

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:12.120
it's because our last guest,
professor of astronautics Dava

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.680
Newman, co-founded EarthDNA It's
a global platform for climate

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.590
advocacy and action.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.320
Our guest today,
Brandon, helped create

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.350
a program called EarthDNA
Ambassadors, which

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.710
provides young people with
climate leadership training.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.290
In addition to learning about
the science behind climate

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.440
change, Ambassadors also
develop skills focused on

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.910
communication, negotiation,
and storytelling.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.250
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
We envision a community

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.360
of climate leaders who
engage their own communities

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.080
on their campuses at their
schools on climate change.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.570
Current Ambassadors come
from different backgrounds,

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:56.040
whether PhD students in
immunology or undergraduate

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.663
students in business.

00:01:57.663 --> 00:01:59.580
Some of them have teaching
experience, but not

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.097
all of them.

00:02:01.097 --> 00:02:02.930
SARAH HANSEN: One of
the tools that EarthDNA

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:07.820
equips their Ambassadors
with is called Climate 101.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:11.600
Available on MIT OpenCourseWare
and EarthDNA.org,

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.400
it's an interactive
workshop that

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.110
was designed to
engage communities

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:18.990
around climate change and
motivate people to act.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.670
But changing human
behavior can be

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.730
difficult, so Climate
101 starts with something

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.840
a bit more approachable--

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.222
the power of conversation.

00:02:28.222 --> 00:02:29.680
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
Well, I think

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.590
talking about it is really
important because I think

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.950
that climate change has
the stigma about it where,

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.590
if you even bring
it up, you're viewed

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:43.030
as a hippie environmentalist
and alarmist and all

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.030
of these things.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.360
And so even by having
conversations about it

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.730
and making it normal
to have conversations

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.670
about climate
change, you empower

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.100
others to do that, as well.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.652
And that's huge because,
for a lot of people,

00:02:57.652 --> 00:02:59.860
they just don't hear about
climate change that often,

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.780
or if they hear about
it, it's not necessarily

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.170
the best information.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:06.430
And so if we can have people
talking about climate change

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.490
in a way that's personal and
meaningful to their family

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:11.950
members, who might
be climate skeptics,

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:15.490
that's one of the best
ways to create the attitude

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:19.630
and behavioral change that's
necessary to actually overcome

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:22.323
the problem of climate change.

00:03:22.323 --> 00:03:24.490
SARAH HANSEN: In addition
to normalizing discussions

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.670
on climate change, the
Climate 101 presentation

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.640
is designed to improve
climate literacy.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.820
That means empowering people to
think critically about climate

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.510
change data and research,
and even targeted marketing

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:38.445
campaigns.

00:03:38.445 --> 00:03:40.070
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
One of the things

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:43.160
that we actually talk about
in the Climate 101 workshop is

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.610
the effect of
different information

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:50.030
campaigns by fossil
fuel companies, who

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.570
have a very clear interest in
preventing any kind of climate

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.840
action and in promoting
misinformation

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.280
among the public,
promoting doubt

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.650
among the public about whether
climate change happens.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.470
It's really important
for me that people

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.140
are able to sift through
all that competing

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:11.760
information in order to
decide for themselves

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.920
whether climate change is real,
whether it matters to them,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.458
and whether they should
do something about it.

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:21.750
Now, I think the answer to
all three of those questions

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.410
is yes, but I want
to help people

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.440
process this deluge of
information by themselves

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.830
and be aware that there are
people that are intentionally

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.465
trying to misinform them.

00:04:34.465 --> 00:04:35.840
SARAH HANSEN:
Climate literacy is

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.050
critical to efforts for
creating a grassroots movement

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.000
of climate change action.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.310
Brandon sees the
EarthDNA Ambassadors,

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.890
who are between the
ages of 16 and 30,

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:49.690
as the next generation of
climate leaders and educators.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:52.190
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: The thing
about teaching young people,

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.500
in particular, is
that young people

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.710
are open-minded to new
material, but they're

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:00.890
mature enough to understand
fairly complex concepts.

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:03.720
They're also part of their
parents' trusted networks.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.910
So if we can talk
to young people,

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:07.760
they can talk to their parents.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.130
Ultimately, we hope
that Ambassadors

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.520
will teach students,
students will teach families,

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.430
and families will demand action.

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:19.490
Ultimately, I think in
creating EarthDNA Ambassadors,

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:21.170
I was really
interested in helping

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:23.780
people who are passionate
about climate change

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.670
find their own
voice, and helping

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:29.510
them to express their own
beliefs and their own opinions

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:32.660
and make a change in
their own community.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.360
I think empowering people
who care about climate change

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.420
to find their voice and use
it has been one of the most

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.760
rewarding things that
I've done, and I'm just

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.530
really grateful
for the opportunity

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:46.250
to be part of this community
of people that are all

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:48.710
interested in self-development,
in leadership,

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.590
and in climate change.

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:53.870
SARAH HANSEN: In putting
the Climate 101 presentation

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.510
together, Brandon
and the EarthDNA team

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:59.060
didn't want to entirely
reinvent the wheel.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.790
They were open to utilizing
many different materials,

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:04.040
from a variety of
reputable sources,

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.310
to create a comprehensive
experience for participants.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:09.358
And part of what
makes it so effective

00:06:09.358 --> 00:06:11.150
is that the presentation
has been developed

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:13.520
to appeal to a wide
audience, whether you're

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.738
a high school student or a
late career professional.

00:06:17.738 --> 00:06:19.280
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
So most climate

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:22.190
change educational materials
focus almost exclusively

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:26.600
on the science, while Climate
101 covers in 45 minutes

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:30.350
the science, the economics, and
the civics of climate change.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.410
And what that allows
us to do is to engage

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:34.880
with people who
wouldn't typically

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.060
consider climate change to be
one of their first priorities.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.730
The idea was to create a
single composition that

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:43.400
keeps people's attention,
that keeps people engaged,

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.860
that keeps people
interested and excited,

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:50.120
while also sharing a large
amount of information

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.980
about climate change from
different perspectives.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.940
And I think that's what
Climate 101 has accomplished.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.790
SARAH HANSEN: And EarthDNA
has data to back that up.

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:01.970
Surveys they've collected show
that, before the presentation,

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:03.570
about half of the
students talked

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:07.070
to their friends or family about
climate change twice a week.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:08.960
But afterward, the
number of people

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.880
participating in
those conversations

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:12.950
jumped to over 80%.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:16.138
Sometimes, those conversations
can be difficult,

00:07:16.138 --> 00:07:17.930
especially when they're
with people who are

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:20.270
skeptical about climate change.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:24.440
But Climate 101 helps
Ambassadors with that, too.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.690
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: When
it comes to climate change,

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.250
even the stories
of what's happening

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:29.540
is very different, right?

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:31.280
We have different
interpretations

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.272
of different facts that lead
us to different conclusions,

00:07:34.272 --> 00:07:36.230
and then we often argue
about those conclusions

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.313
without understanding each
other's interpretations

00:07:38.313 --> 00:07:40.860
or the observations that
each of us have made.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.450
And so even in bringing up a
climate change conversation,

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:47.720
you have to be aware that you
might be threatening the way

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.430
that somebody sees themselves.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:51.860
Or maybe in engaging
in these conversations,

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:56.360
you are threatening somebody's
sense of patriotism or sense

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:58.980
of being a good person.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:01.040
Especially in areas
where the economy

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:04.220
is based in fossil fuels,
it can be really difficult

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:06.740
because, ultimately, a
climate change conversation

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:10.310
can amount to an accusation
that that person is not just

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:13.820
providing food for their family,
but also is damaging the Earth

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.840
and harming a lot of people.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:19.190
And so it's really important
to consider all of those things

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:22.190
as you have these climate
change conversations.

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:25.490
So instead of trying to
convince the other person

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:29.360
or persuade the
other person, listen,

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.080
help them empty their
heart and express why

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:34.940
they're frustrated
with the situation.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:36.679
And then they'll be
more likely to listen

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:40.730
to your own experiences
and more receptive to why

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:43.745
you think climate change
matters and is relevant.

00:08:46.975 --> 00:08:48.350
SARAH HANSEN:
Something else that

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:50.510
helps the Climate
101 presentation

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:52.910
resonate across
different audiences

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:56.150
is the incorporation
of local insights.

00:08:56.150 --> 00:08:59.000
This targeted approach
helps distill the larger,

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.700
sometimes overwhelming
issue of climate change

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:04.268
into something more digestible.

00:09:04.268 --> 00:09:06.810
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: One of
the problems with climate change

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:10.860
discussions is that
it's a huge problem,

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:13.510
right, it affects
the entire world,

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:15.780
and that happens over
the course of decades.

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:19.950
But people live their lives
at home or in their town,

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:23.440
and people live their
lives day to day.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:26.790
So when you start talking about
these massive, overwhelming

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:29.700
changes, it can be really
easy to just dismiss them

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:32.430
as either things
that don't affect me

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.550
or things that I can't
do anything about.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:39.010
The idea of creating localized
insights and personally

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:41.257
meaningful insights
is that we need

00:09:41.257 --> 00:09:43.090
to talk about climate
change in a way that's

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:46.250
relevant to the
intended audience.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:49.660
And so if we can say, for
example, well, here in Boston,

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:53.290
the city was actually
considering building seawalls

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:56.212
because of anticipated
flooding, now, all of a sudden,

00:09:56.212 --> 00:09:58.420
that's something that's
happening in my neighborhood.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:01.810
That's something that's like,
oh my god, that's coming for me.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:05.590
That's not the Earth is warming
by 2 degrees Celsius, which

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:07.280
doesn't really mean
much to me, it's,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:10.395
oh my god, there's going
to be flooding in Boston?

00:10:10.395 --> 00:10:12.270
SARAH HANSEN: The more
I talked with Brandon,

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.670
the more I wanted to
know what was personally

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:18.240
motivating him to engage
in climate action work.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.480
He began to talk about the
intergenerational experiences

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:23.590
of his own family.

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:25.590
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: A
few years ago, my family

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:29.550
celebrated its 25th anniversary
in the United States.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:32.400
We were all at my
grandparents' place, seated

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.040
around this beautiful
dining room table in front

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:38.100
of a gorgeous display
case full of fancy china,

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.930
and my grandma was
bustling around,

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:43.290
making sure we all had enough
of that fatty and meaty

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:46.230
Ukrainian food that
my family loves.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:48.940
And my family was
telling stories,

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:51.840
stories of my childhood that
I'd been hearing for years,

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.960
but stories that helped us
reflect on what it meant

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.910
to come to the United States.

00:10:56.910 --> 00:10:59.400
My grandpa told
how he'd only taken

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:01.770
one flight in his
whole life, and it

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:04.030
was to come to
the United States,

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:06.580
because the only thing that
he feared more than flying

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:09.850
was staying in a
collapsed Soviet state.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:14.800
My grandma, how she came to the
US without knowing any English,

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:17.740
and not only learned English,
but also learned how to code,

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:21.060
and recently retired as a
senior software developer.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.780
My mom, how she, after
four months here,

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:28.740
was caught stealing a
$7 bottle of face cream

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:31.200
because she wanted to get a
gift for her mom's birthday

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.480
and she couldn't afford it.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:36.240
And my mom went on and started
a business that ended up

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.120
putting me through college,
but even 25 years later, she

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:43.600
was still so embarrassed
and ashamed of that moment.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:46.750
I turned to my dad, and I
asked him whether he ever

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:48.820
missed the Ukraine.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:49.940
It's where he grew up.

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:52.090
It's where his friends are.

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:55.120
And he turned to me and
responded in Russian

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.250
that only in his
worst nightmare would

00:11:57.250 --> 00:11:59.990
he ever wake up
back in that hell.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:03.350
And to me, that moment
really crystallizes this idea

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:05.960
that so much of
who we are and what

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:08.780
we have is determined
by choices that people

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:10.580
make before we're even born.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:14.570
And that's just such
a great injustice.

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:18.320
And climate change
exemplifies that.

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:21.170
I think climate change is
one of the great injustices

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:22.160
of our time.

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:26.510
It was created primarily by
wealthy, privileged people

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:29.600
in developed nations,
and it will primarily

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:34.490
affect the vulnerable, the
poor, the sick, in many cases

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:37.310
people who never even
contributed to the problem.

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:40.490
I think that, for those of
us who have an opportunity

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:43.490
to fight that injustice, for
those of us who have benefited

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:47.450
from being fortunate
and from being born

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:49.590
in the right place
at the right time,

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:53.600
we have a real obligation
to help the people who

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:56.000
don't necessarily have
those opportunities now

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:58.070
reach those same opportunities.

00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:01.370
And in this case, it
means doing something

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:05.030
about this massive global
problem that will eventually

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:07.550
affect all of us, but
is already affecting

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:09.945
many of the most vulnerable.

00:13:15.038 --> 00:13:16.580
SARAH HANSEN: One
important component

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:19.550
in tackling climate
change is recognizing

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:21.890
that it's a collective
issue that affects just

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:23.490
about everyone.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:27.050
Brandon incorporated interactive
activities into the Climate 101

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:30.440
curriculum to help simulate
how these collective dilemmas

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:32.150
can play out.

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:34.670
These activities are
modeled on the concept

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:37.550
of the tragedy of the
commons, where people tend

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:39.800
to act selfishly
when they believe

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:43.250
that their individual
actions won't make an impact.

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:45.950
A classic example of the
tragedy of the commons

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:49.160
is the gradual overfishing
of a public waterway.

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:51.320
And in some ways,
this concept mirrors

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:54.465
what we've seen throughout
the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:13:54.465 --> 00:13:56.090
While these issues
are quite different,

00:13:56.090 --> 00:13:59.540
of course, they do both require
collaborative efforts to make

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:01.580
a real difference
in tackling them,

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:03.380
and our everyday
choices actually

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:06.840
can have an immediate impact.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:10.040
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: I think
COVID is a really great example

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:12.860
of collective dilemmas.

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:14.900
And in the US, in
particular, we have

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:17.990
this very individualist
attitude, personal liberty

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:18.980
above all else.

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:22.220
And personal liberty is
obviously super important,

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:27.230
but we see in a case like
COVID that, when crises arise,

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:30.740
sometimes we do need to act
for the collective good.

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:32.580
And that can be
really difficult,

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:34.520
but climate change is similar.

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:37.490
The longer we waited to
take action on COVID,

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:39.860
the more drastic the
action had to be.

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:41.680
And with climate
change, it's similar.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:45.920
Had we started really focusing
on reducing our emissions

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:48.470
and transitioning
to renewable energy,

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:50.990
as well as making some of the
other changes we need to make,

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:55.040
in 2000, we could have
done it pretty gradually,

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:56.870
and at a reasonable pace.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:15:01.040
But we waited 20 years, and it
looks like we're still waiting.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:03.470
So now, the action
that we have to take

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:08.460
is much more drastic than
had we done it 20 years ago.

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:10.220
And if we continue
to wait, that action

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:12.770
will have to be
still more drastic.

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:15.980
So yesterday is better
than today, and today

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:18.620
is better than tomorrow, to
quote one of the articles

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:21.228
that I read regarding
COVID, but that also applies

00:15:21.228 --> 00:15:22.520
equally well to climate change.

00:15:24.853 --> 00:15:26.770
SARAH HANSEN: Many people
worry that attention

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:30.700
on climate change has weakened
due to the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:33.100
but Brandon sees a
space for optimism,

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:36.437
and views these overlapping
crises as an opportunity.

00:15:36.437 --> 00:15:39.020
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: I think,
for a lot of climate activists,

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:43.280
right now is difficult in
terms of climate activism

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:46.340
because COVID is a really
serious and really urgent

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:48.500
problem, and it can
be hard to spare

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:50.900
any attention or any
emotion for climate

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:53.090
change, this other scary thing.

00:15:53.090 --> 00:15:56.220
But times of crisis are
actually times of opportunity.

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:59.090
And so right now is
a time that people

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:03.510
feel like they need connection
and purpose and community.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:05.300
And so right now is
an opportunity for us

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:08.330
to continue growing and
giving people those things,

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:11.510
and it's also an opportunity
to fight even harder for some

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:13.730
of the changes that we
need to make in order

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:16.370
to overcome the climate
change issue, as well

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:18.795
as the COVID issue.

00:16:18.795 --> 00:16:21.420
SARAH HANSEN: Much of Brandon's
work around this project, which

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:23.070
inspires others to
take a leadership

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:26.550
role in their own way, has been
driven by his own experiences

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:28.500
with leaders in his life.

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:30.330
Throughout the
COVID-19 pandemic,

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:33.420
he's found strong leaders in his
advisor, Professor Dava Newman

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:37.080
at MIT, who you heard from
in our last episode, and Rand

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:39.570
Wentworth, Brandon's
environmental leadership

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:42.590
professor at the
Harvard Kennedy School.

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:44.090
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
They have both

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:46.850
been such incredible
models of what it means

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:49.430
to be a leader during a crisis.

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:53.580
They have been hyperattentive
to their students' needs,

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:57.050
so empathetic, such
good listeners,

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.620
adaptive and flexible
and positive.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:04.130
And that has been
really inspiring for me.

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:07.780
Empathy is everything, and
it's just so hard right now.

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:11.599
You know, my emotional
reserves that I usually

00:17:11.599 --> 00:17:14.240
use to accomplish tasks
that I don't necessarily

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:16.460
find as interesting are gone.

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:18.650
At this point, I'm
just hyperfocused

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:22.040
on staying sane, staying
happy, staying healthy.

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:26.359
And Rand and Dava both
really understand that.

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:29.390
And they start every
meeting with a check-in.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:30.560
How are you guys?

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:31.820
What can I do to help?

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:35.150
And that's something that
I hope to take forward

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:39.690
as an immediate response in
a crisis, is to figure out,

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:43.040
OK, how are the people that are
affected by this crisis doing,

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:45.320
and what can I do
to accommodate them

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:48.555
and to make it easier for them.

00:17:48.555 --> 00:17:49.930
SARAH HANSEN:
Brandon understands

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:53.710
that many people, when faced
with the COVID-19 pandemic as

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:55.330
well as the threat
of climate change,

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:58.810
are experiencing a
kind of doom fatigue.

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:01.540
If you seek it out, there
can be an overwhelming amount

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:03.820
of bad news about both crises.

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:06.460
But Brandon is working on
reframing this thinking

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:08.860
into something more
approachable so that people

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:10.810
can carry forward
with important work

00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:14.260
in their own manageable way,
without feeling like it's all

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:15.610
just too much to handle.

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:17.360
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
There are definitely

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:21.830
times when I also feel like,
why am I even bothering?

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:26.750
There's so much out
there to improve.

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:29.230
What can I do?

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:33.130
There's a story that I love.

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:36.160
It's told by Wangari
Maathai, who's

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:40.270
a Nobel Peace Prize winner
for her environmental work.

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:44.560
She tells a story of a
great big forest fire,

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:49.030
and how all of the animals
are running out of the forest,

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:52.060
and they're standing
next to it as it burns.

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:57.250
But one animal, the hummingbird,
starts going to a nearby lake

00:18:57.250 --> 00:19:00.490
and carrying one drop
of water at a time

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:02.800
to douse the forest fire.

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:05.950
And all the other animals are
asking, what are you doing?

00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:07.090
You're so small.

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:08.170
This fire is so big.

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:11.580
You can't possibly
do anything about it.

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:14.850
The elephant is standing
there with its big trunk,

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:17.430
all the other animals that
are also much bigger, just

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:18.780
doing nothing.

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:20.970
The hummingbird keeps on going.

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:23.730
And then finally,
without missing a beat,

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:28.170
the hummingbird says,
I'm doing the best I can.

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:30.750
And Wangari Maathai
says, we should all

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:32.820
be like hummingbirds.

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:35.520
We should all just do
the best that we can.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.590
And some days, that's
not very much at all.

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:40.050
And that's OK.

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:44.250
But we should all remember
that we each can do something,

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:47.550
and that that matters, and that
that's enough in that moment.

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:58.330
SARAH HANSEN: In our individual
work on climate change,

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:00.790
it's OK to take the
approach of the hummingbird

00:20:00.790 --> 00:20:02.480
to make a difference.

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:05.920
If you're interested in becoming
an EarthDNA Ambassador or want

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:08.990
